import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Overview — where the kernel work is, and the M1 plan","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Overview — where the kernel work is, and the M1 plan","type":"report","date":"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/reports/2026-10-27-overview-m1.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/reports/2026-10-27-overview-m1.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/reports/2026-10-27-overview-m1.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="overview-—-where-the-kernel-work-is-and-the-m1-plan" tabindex="-1">Overview — where the kernel work is, and the M1 plan <a class="header-anchor" href="#overview-—-where-the-kernel-work-is-and-the-m1-plan" aria-label="Permalink to “Overview — where the kernel work is, and the M1 plan”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-10-27 · for review</p><h2 id="_0-·-refactor-context-adr-0159-adr-0160" tabindex="-1">0 · Refactor context (ADR-0159, ADR-0160) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_0-·-refactor-context-adr-0159-adr-0160" aria-label="Permalink to “0 · Refactor context (ADR-0159, ADR-0160)”">​</a></h2><p>The domains keep their lab form for now, but the naming and the endpoint are settled:</p><ul><li><strong>ADR-0159</strong> — the optimizers become <code>hnl optimize --domain graph|kernel</code> commands (<code>ext-*</code>) once three triggers hold: a caller that is not the researcher, a surface unchanged for four weeks, and a shared referee. Wrap the Python core behind a port; <strong>do not rewrite</strong> it in Rust.</li><li><strong>Naming</strong> — the family is <code>opt-*</code>: <code>opt-graph</code> (today <code>graphopt</code>), <code>opt-kernel</code> (today <code>kernelopt</code>). A third member (<code>opt-schedule</code>) only on the rule of three.</li><li><strong>ADR-0160</strong> (allocated) — the kernel IR is <code>Term</code> + kit-declared ops; the six combinators stay closed.</li><li><strong>Generic core stays a library</strong> — <code>egraph</code>, <code>discovery</code>, <code>eqsat</code> are domain-free; a domain is rules + cost model + adapters.</li></ul><p>Everything below is <strong>lab-form</strong>: repos, drivers, own harnesses. No ext wrapper, no FFI seam, until the triggers fire.</p><h2 id="_1-·-the-thesis" tabindex="-1">1 · The thesis <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-·-the-thesis" aria-label="Permalink to “1 · The thesis”">​</a></h2><p>Hand-tuning kernels is expensive, so it is only bought for popular shapes on popular hardware. A machine that proposes, prices and certifies kernels can cover the long tail. The moat is not one fast kernel — it is the referee (honest measurement), the collapsing cost per experiment, and certification.</p><h2 id="_2-·-what-exists-today" tabindex="-1">2 · What exists today <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-·-what-exists-today" aria-label="Permalink to “2 · What exists today”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>layer</th><th>what</th><th>state</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>kernel</code> / <code>engine</code> / <code>host</code></td><td>closed six-combinator calculus, engines, plugin host</td><td>working</td></tr><tr><td><code>kernelopt</code></td><td>IR, <code>KERNEL_RULES</code>, sink, cost model, sieve, minting</td><td>working; composes, rarely invents</td></tr><tr><td><code>graphopt</code></td><td>LLM-graph optimizer, 6,291 tests, evidence ledger</td><td>working</td></tr><tr><td><code>graphopt/bench/benchkit/regime.py</code></td><td>deploy-regime referee (eviction, blocked rounds, roofline)</td><td><strong>new, tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>graphopt/bench/drivers/probes/awq/</code></td><td>the int4 kernel + all P13–P18 probes</td><td><strong>new, demo-grade</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="_3-·-what-this-session-proved" tabindex="-1">3 · What this session proved <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-·-what-this-session-proved" aria-label="Permalink to “3 · What this session proved”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Kernel margin (valid):</strong> our int4 GEMV beats <code>awq_gemm</code> cold on three of four layer shapes — <strong>2.96× / 1.52× / 1.35×</strong>, exact.</li><li><strong>It reaches the model (valid):</strong> substituted into vLLM with the checkpoint tensors snapshotted before repacking, output is <strong>token-identical</strong> and the model is <strong>29.2 % faster</strong> (1164.6 → 824.1 ms, 25/25 paired rounds, decode, one 4 GB laptop GPU).</li><li><strong>Three retractions:</strong> a fake 3.3× (fp16 atomics), a fake −3.1 % (too few rounds), a fake −28.5 % (wrong AWQ nibble order). All caught by the harness, all published as retractions.</li><li><strong>The bug worth remembering:</strong> AWQ packs 8 int4 per int32 in the order <code>[0,4,1,5,2,6,3,7]</code>. My reference used the same wrong order, so it validated itself. <em>A self-written reference proves self-consistency, not correctness.</em></li></ul><p><strong>What is still not true: the machine did not find that kernel — I wrote it.</strong> It swept 48 configs of my design. Closing that gap is M1.</p><h2 id="_4-·-the-m1-plan-five-items-5-days" tabindex="-1">4 · The M1 plan (five items, ~5 days) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-·-the-m1-plan-five-items-5-days" aria-label="Permalink to “4 · The M1 plan (five items, ~5 days)”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>build</th><th>home</th><th>gate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>ADR:</strong> kernel IR enters as data, kernel stays closed</td><td><code>kernelopt</code></td><td>accepted</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong><code>lib-kernel-ir</code></strong> — ops, grades, costs, denotations (a handl <strong>lib</strong>)</td><td>new repo</td><td>every op has a signature, grade, cost, reference</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong><code>compile-triton</code></strong> — Rust engine: <code>Term</code> → Triton, <code>qgemv</code> × 4 <code>split_k</code></td><td>new repo</td><td>4 variants compile and run</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Equality gate vs <code>awq_gemm</code></strong></td><td><code>graphopt/bench</code></td><td>cosine ≥ 0.9999, all variants</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Search driver</strong> in <code>kernelopt</code></td><td><code>kernelopt</code></td><td>emits a best kernel + routing row</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Visibility (ADR-0150):</strong> <code>lib-kernel-ir</code> public; <code>compile-triton</code>, the runtime binding and any <code>ext-opt-*</code> are tooling — private until done.</p><p><strong>M1 gate:</strong> four variants generated from <code>Term</code>s, all exact, and at least one within the hand kernel&#39;s <strong>58.9 µs cold</strong> on <code>down_proj</code>. <strong>Kill:</strong> if a fixed lowering cannot match hand-written Triton on one operator, generalising is wasted work — keep hand-writing kernels and use the search only for configs.</p><h2 id="_5-·-architecture-corrected-after-review" tabindex="-1">5 · Architecture (corrected after review) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-·-architecture-corrected-after-review" aria-label="Permalink to “5 · Architecture (corrected after review)”">​</a></h2><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>lib-kernel-ir  ──Terms (kit-declared ops)──▶  kernelopt  ──term──▶  compile-triton (Rust)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span> (vocabulary,                                (enumerate, gate,          (Term → Triton)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  data)                                       price, choose)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                                                  │ chosen</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                                                  ▼</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                                        graphopt/bench (referee)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                                                  │ winner</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                                                  ▼</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                                        lib-triton-qgemv  (M2)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Three corrections that shaped it: a <strong>kit is a manifest of libs</strong> (so <code>lib-kernel-ir</code> + <code>kit-kernel-ir</code>, not one thing); <code>compile-triton</code> is <strong>Rust</strong> like the rest of the family; and <strong>there is no new schema — the IR is <code>Term</code></strong>, so certificate, sink, sieve and cost model all apply unchanged.</p><p><strong>Two-stage schedule, stated for attribution:</strong> our IR picks blocks and split; Triton picks pipelining, vectorization and layouts.</p><h2 id="_6-·-risks-and-what-they-cost" tabindex="-1">6 · Risks, and what they cost <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-·-risks-and-what-they-cost" aria-label="Permalink to “6 · Risks, and what they cost”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>risk</th><th>counter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Triton codegen can&#39;t match hand-written Triton</td><td>M1 kill criterion — cheap to learn</td></tr><tr><td>cost model prunes winners</td><td>used only as a hard bound, never to rank</td></tr><tr><td>search rate-limited by compilation</td><td>compile cache before scaling past ~10³ candidates</td></tr><tr><td>overfitting to one GPU</td><td>every routing table ships with its calibration</td></tr><tr><td>silent wrongness</td><td>equality gate vs the <strong>incumbent</strong>, inside the timing path</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="_7-·-decisions-resolved-2026-10-27" tabindex="-1">7 · Decisions (resolved 2026-10-27) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-·-decisions-resolved-2026-10-27" aria-label="Permalink to “7 · Decisions (resolved 2026-10-27)”">​</a></h2><p><strong>1. M1 covers two operators:</strong> <code>qgemv</code> <strong>and</strong> <code>gate_proj</code> — the second has the opposite geometry (large N, small K), so the space has to generalise rather than overfit one shape. Gate for <code>qgemv</code>: within the hand kernel&#39;s 58.9 µs cold. Gate for <code>gate_proj</code>: no worse than today&#39;s <code>awq_gemm</code> (57.8 µs), i.e. at least 0.95× — it is the operator we currently lose, and turning a loss into a tie is the honest first target.</p><p><strong>2. Public, and the names change: there is no &quot;cuda&quot; in this design.</strong> Triton targets NVIDIA <em>and</em> AMD, and the IR carries resource grades rather than assuming one vendor. So:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>old</th><th>new</th><th>why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>lib-kernel-ir</code></td><td><strong><code>lib-kernel-ir</code></strong></td><td>it is a kernel IR, hardware-agnostic</td></tr><tr><td><code>kit-kernel-ir</code></td><td><strong><code>kit-kernel-ir</code></strong></td><td>kit = manifest of libs</td></tr><tr><td><code>lib-triton-qgemv</code> (M2)</td><td><strong><code>lib-triton-qgemv</code></strong></td><td>the runtime binding executes Triton, not CUDA</td></tr><tr><td><code>compile-triton</code></td><td>unchanged</td><td>names the <em>target language</em>, which is accurate</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The device class (<code>sm_count</code>, bandwidth, L2) enters as <strong>grade data</strong>, not as a name, so a second vendor is a calibration, not a rename.</p><p><strong>3. The ADR lives in <code>kernelopt</code></strong> — the domain that owns the IR and the search records the decision it depends on.</p><p><strong>4. Ambition: both breadth and depth.</strong> That has one consequence for sequencing: depth first (two operators proven end to end), then breadth (formats × shapes), because breadth multiplies the candidate count and therefore <strong>requires the compile cache before it, not after</strong>.</p><h3 id="amended-m1-boundary" tabindex="-1">Amended M1 boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#amended-m1-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to “Amended M1 boundary”">​</a></h3><p><strong>In:</strong> ADR (<code>kernelopt</code>) · <code>lib-kernel-ir</code> with nine ops + denotations · <code>compile-triton</code> for <code>qgemv</code> <strong>and</strong> <code>gate_proj</code> (4 <code>split_k</code> variants each) · equality gate vs <code>awq_gemm</code> · search driver.</p><p><strong>No kit in M1.</strong> A kit exists to assemble several libs; with one lib there is nothing to assemble. <code>kit-kernel-ir</code> arrives at M2, when the <code>lib-triton-qgemv</code> binding, the cost tables and the base types have to meet. (Same reason <code>kit-prelude-lang</code> exists and a single lib does not.)</p><p><strong>M1.5 (before breadth):</strong> compile cache + candidate pruning by roofline bound. Without it, breadth is rate-limited by <code>triton.jit</code>, not by ideas.</p><p><strong>Still out:</strong> <code>lib-triton-qgemv</code> binding, FP8/GPTQ, <code>compile-cuda</code>.</p>`,35)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};